---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Dispatch from '../../components/Dispatch.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site.js';
---

<Base
  title="Black Algae in Texas Pools: Causes & Real Fix"
  description="Black algae roots into plaster where normal chlorine can't reach it. Why it keeps coming back in Central Texas pools and what actually clears it. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>Black algae in Texas pools: why it comes back and what actually kills it</h1>

  <p>
    Black algae keeps coming back because it doesn't just sit on the surface like green algae —
    it roots into plaster and grout lines, growing a protective layer that ordinary chlorine
    levels can't penetrate. Killing it takes aggressive brushing to break that layer open before
    chemical treatment can reach the organism underneath, not just a heavier dose of shock.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Dark spots in the pool that brush off and return" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>How it gets into a Central Texas pool</h2>
  <p>
    Black algae is a common hitchhiker on swimsuits, floats, and pool toys that have been in
    natural water — Lake Georgetown, Lake Belton, and Stillhouse Hollow are all popular weekend
    spots, and gear that goes from lake to backyard pool is one of the most reliable ways this
    algae gets introduced into water that was previously clean. It can also arrive from a
    contaminated pool brush or vacuum shared between properties.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it survives normal treatment</h2>
  <p>
    Green and mustard algae float or cling loosely to surfaces, so a good shock dose reaches them
    directly. Black algae is different: it anchors into porous plaster and forms a protective
    outer layer, effectively hiding most of the organism from the water around it. A normal
    chlorine level, and even a normal shock dose, mostly just treats the surface layer while the
    root survives underneath — which is exactly why it looks dead, then reappears in the same
    spot days later.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually works</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Brush hard, and brush the right way.</strong> A stainless-steel bristle brush
      worked directly against each spot, breaking the protective layer physically, has to happen
      before chemical treatment can do much good.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Treat immediately after brushing.</strong> Once the layer is broken, chlorine needs
      to reach the exposed organism before it can reseal itself — timing between brushing and
      treatment matters.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Repeat over several days.</strong> One aggressive session rarely gets every spot
      completely; black algae treatment is typically a multi-day process of brush, treat, and
      recheck rather than a single visit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check circulation and filtration are actually working</strong> throughout
      treatment — see our guide on{' '}
      <a href="/guides/pool-green-but-pump-running/">green pools with a healthy pump</a> if
      you're unsure whether your equipment is pulling its weight during recovery.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why early treatment matters so much here</h2>
  <p>
    A few dark spots caught early is a contained, fast job. Left alone, black algae spreads
    across more of the plaster surface and each colony gets a longer head start on rooting in —
    which turns a short brushing session into a much longer recovery. If you're seeing spots
    that brush off and come right back, that pattern alone is worth acting on rather than
    waiting to see if it spreads.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    Spot-brushing a small area yourself is safe to try, but widespread black algae, or algae that
    keeps returning after a few DIY attempts, usually means the treatment isn't reaching the root
    layer consistently enough. That is a targeted job, not a bigger dose of the same approach —
    {SITE.feeLine}
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Is black algae dangerous to swim near?</h3>
    <p>
      It's primarily a water-quality and pool-surface problem rather than a direct health hazard
      in the way raw sewage contamination would be, but a pool actively fighting black algae is
      worth clearing before regular swimming resumes, since the chemical balance during
      treatment isn't typical swim-ready water.
    </p>
    <h3>Can black algae damage the pool surface permanently?</h3>
    <p>
      Prolonged, untreated colonies can etch into plaster more deeply over time, making future
      treatment harder and occasionally leaving a visible stain even after the algae itself is
      gone. Earlier treatment reduces that risk significantly.
    </p>
    <h3>Will a normal chlorine increase prevent black algae from coming back?</h3>
    <p>
      It helps but isn't a guarantee, since new colonies are usually introduced from outside the
      pool (lake gear, shared equipment) rather than growing from nothing in well-balanced water.
      Rinsing gear that's been in natural water before it goes in the pool is a simple prevention
      step.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Dark spots in the pool that brush off and return" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
